"The European Rights Retention Community of Practice (CoP) brings together experts and practitioners across Europe to exchange experiences, learn from one another, and advance rights retention strategies.

For our 11th meeting, we are pleased to welcome institutional case studies from Northern and Southern Europe. Aysa Ekanger and Bror-Magnus S. Strand from UiT The Arctic University of Norway—one of Northern Europe's early adopters—and Ignasi Labastida from the University of Barcelona, a recent leader in Southern Europe, will present their institutional processes and progress in adopting rights retention policies. Following the presentations, we will share key updates on the Rights Retention in Practice task force's work. We then invite community members to share their own experiences, highlight ongoing challenges, and collaborate on practical solutions. &nbsp;

If you work at an academic institution, are a funder, or an open science policymaker, please join us to contribute to the conversation."

"The term “preprint” is widely used in scholarly communication, but it does not have an agreed-upon definition across disciplines, platforms, or professional roles.&nbsp;

The National Information Standards Organization (NISO) broadly defines a preprint as a scholarly article made publicly available before it has been peer reviewed, accepted for publication, or formally endorsed. Yet NISO also recognizes that some preprints are never submitted to a traditional publication venue, while others undergo community or other forms of review before being posted.[1]

New forms of feedback complicate this picture further. For example, bioRxiv and medRxiv have endorsed two AI peer-review services, Q.E.D. and Reviewer3, that allow authors to receive AI feedback either before or after posting their preprints.[2]"
